Subject: [PATCH] netinfo: network quality from the profile

navigator.connection effectiveType, rtt, downlink and saveData come from
the profile's network section, as do the rtt, downlink and ect client
hints, the Save-Data header and prefers-reduced-data. The values go into
Blink's stored network state, so Chromium's own rounding and per-host
noise apply and the headers match JavaScript. The round trip is clamped
to the band for the claimed connection type, so a profile cannot pair 4g
with a 2G latency. DevTools network emulation overrides the profile as
usual, and a profile without a network section leaves the host's link in
place.

--- a/base/apostate/profile.h
+++ b/base/apostate/profile.h
@@ -396,6 +396,38 @@
     return accept_languages_;
   }
 
+  // The network the claimed device is on, as the Network Information API
+  // reports it.
+  //
+  // effective_type is spelled the way the JS enum spells it: "slow-2g", "2g",
+  // "3g" or "4g". http_rtt_ms and downlink_mbps are the raw estimates and not
+  // the numbers a page reads. NetworkStateNotifier::RoundRtt and RoundMbps
+  // multiply by a per-host salted 0.90-1.10 and then quantise to 50 ms and
+  // 50 kbps, capping at 3 s and 10 Mbps, so the observable value is always
+  // produced by Chromium's own arithmetic. Storing the estimate rather than
+  // the rounded answer is what keeps that true.
+  //
+  // A named type and a round trip pin each other: the emitter holds the round
+  // trip to the band Chromium's own thresholds give that type, and derives the
+  // type from the round trip when only one of the two is present. So a profile
+  // cannot present "4g" with a 700 ms round trip however it was written.
+  const std::optional<std::string>& network_effective_type() const {
+    return network_effective_type_;
+  }
+  std::optional<int> network_http_rtt_ms() const {
+    return network_http_rtt_ms_;
+  }
+  std::optional<double> network_downlink_mbps() const {
+    return network_downlink_mbps_;
+  }
+
+  // Whether the user has data saver on. One value with four emitters:
+  // navigator.connection.saveData, the Save-Data request header, the
+  // prefers-reduced-data media feature, and Blink's own loading heuristics.
+  // Desktop Chrome has no UI that turns this on, so a desktop profile that
+  // sets it true is claiming something desktop Chrome cannot produce.
+  std::optional<bool> network_save_data() const { return network_save_data_; }
+
   // Pointing device capability: "fine", "coarse" or "none".
   //
   // Headless has no input device at all, so it reports none — and a desktop
@@ -477,6 +509,10 @@
   std::optional<std::string> timezone_;
